[
  {
    "title": "Unit 1: Key Vocabulary & Terminology (Part 1) 📚",
    "content": "📌\n<b>Welcome Freshmen Students to Communicative English Skills I (EnLa101)! 👋✨</b>\n\nHere is the complete vocabulary list for Unit 1: Life Skills (Passage-Based), formatted clearly for study and exam preparation.\n\n<b>1. Interpersonal (Adjective)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Relating to relationships or communication between people.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> የሰዎች እርስ በርስ የግንኙነት/የመግባባት\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Social, Relational, Interactive\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Personal, Individual, Intrapersonal\n• <b>Example:</b> Life skills help students build effective interpersonal relationships with peers.\n\n<b>2. Generic (Adjective)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Characteristic of or relating to a class or group of things; general.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> አጠቃላይ፣ የወል\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> General, Comprehensive, Universal\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Specific, Particular, Exclusive\n• <b>Example:</b> Problem-solving and decision-making are generic life skills applicable anywhere.\n\n<b>3. Competently (Adverb)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> In an efficient and capable way.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> በብቃት፣ በጥሩ ሁኔታ\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Capably, Efficiently, Skillfully\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Incompetently, Inadequately\n• <b>Example:</b> Students learn life skills to function competently in the wider community.\n\n<b>4. Self-Reliance (Noun)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Reliance on one's own powers and resources rather than those of others.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> በራስ መተማመን/መቆም\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Independence, Autonomy, Self-sufficiency\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Dependency, Reliance\n• <b>Example:</b> Developing self-reliance skills helps students organize their lives effectively."
  },
  {
    "title": "Unit 1: Key Vocabulary & Terminology (Part 2) 📖",
    "content": "📌\n<b>5. Innate (Adjective)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Inborn; natural; present from birth.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> ተፈጥሯዊ፣ አብሮ የተወለደ\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Inborn, Natural, Hereditary\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Acquired, Learned\n• <b>Example:</b> Humans have an innate desire to belong to a group or family.\n\n<b>6. Kinship (Noun)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> A feeling of being close to or connected with other people; blood relationship.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> የሥጋ ዝምድና፣ የቅርበት ስሜት\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Affinity, Connection, Bond\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Alienation, Separation\n• <b>Example:</b> Human nature has a strong potential for kinship and belonging to society.\n\n<b>7. Intrinsically (Adverb)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> In an essential or natural way; belonging naturally.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> ከተፈጥሮው/ከውስጥ የመነጨ\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Inherently, Naturally, Fundamentally\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Extrinsically, Externally\n• <b>Example:</b> Learning ways to trust and care for others is intrinsically rewarding.\n\n<b>8. Prerequisite (Noun)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> A thing that is required as a prior condition for something else to happen.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> ቅድመ-ሁኔታ፣ አስፈላጊ መሠረት\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Requirement, Essential, Precondition\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Option, Nonessential\n• <b>Example:</b> Fulfillment of the need for belonging is a prerequisite for emotional well-being."
  },
  {
    "title": "Unit 1: Key Vocabulary & Terminology (Part 3) 💡",
    "content": "📌\n<b>9. Aloof (Adjective)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Not friendly or forthcoming; cool and distant.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> ከሰው የሚርቅ፣ ገለልተኛ\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Distant, Detached, Unfriendly\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Friendly, Sociable, Welcoming\n• <b>Example:</b> When a person's need for belonging is unmet, they may easily become aloof.\n\n<b>10. Reciprocal (Adjective)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Given, felt, or done in return; mutual.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> የጋራ፣ እርስ በርስ የሚደረግ\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Mutual, Complementary, Shared\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> One-sided, Unilateral\n• <b>Example:</b> Belonging allows individuals to express social interest in a reciprocal manner.\n\n<b>11. Self-Efficacy (Noun)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> An individual's belief in their capacity to execute behaviors necessary to produce specific performance attainments.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> በራስ አቅም ማመን/መተማመን\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Self-confidence, Capability, Assurance\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Insecurity, Self-doubt\n• <b>Example:</b> Mastering life skills contributes to a student's sense of self-efficacy.\n\n<b>12. Innumerable (Adjective)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Too many to be counted.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> ቁጥር የሌለው፣ እጅግ ብዙ\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Countless, Infinite, Numerous\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Finite, Limited, Few\n• <b>Example:</b> The skills that can be categorized as life skills are innumerable."
  },
  {
    "title": "Unit 1: Contextual Vocabulary & Context Meanings (Part 4) 🔑",
    "content": "📌\n<b>13. Core (Noun / Adjective)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> The central or most important part of something.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> ዋና፣ መሠረታዊ\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Central, Essential, Fundamental\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Peripheral, Minor\n• <b>Example:</b> There is a core set of skills at the heart of youth well-being initiatives.\n\n<b>14. Pursue (Verb)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Follow or chase; strive to achieve.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> መከተል፣ ማሳደድ፣ ለመድረስ መጣር\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Seek, Strive for, Follow\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Abandon, Give up\n• <b>Example:</b> Belonging involves the ability to pursue meaningful personal relationships.\n\n<b>15. Peer Pressure (Noun)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Direct influence on people by peers to change their attitudes or behavior.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> የእኩዮች ተፅዕኖ\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Peer influence, Group pressure\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Independence, Self-determination\n• <b>Example:</b> Personal skills help young people say 'no' in the face of negative peer pressure.\n\n<b>16. Framed (Verb / Adjective)</b>\n• <b>English:</b> Formulated, shaped, or expressed in a particular context.\n• <b>Amharic:</b> የተቀረጸ፣ የተገለጸ\n• <b>Synonyms:</b> Formulated, Defined, Expressed\n• <b>Antonyms:</b> Misrepresented, Distortion\n• <b>Example:</b> Life skills are framed as abilities for adaptive and positive behavior."
  },
  {
    "title": "Unit 1: Contextual Reference Words & Final Notes 🎯",
    "content": "📌\n<b>📖 Pronoun Reference Guide (Unit 1 Passage):</b>\n• <b>'these'</b> (Para 3, Line 1) ➔ Refers to <i>life skills that enable people to maximize choices</i>.\n• <b>'they'</b> (Para 3, Line 3) ➔ Refers to <i>people who are being personally responsible</i>.\n• <b>'those'</b> (Para 5, Line 6) ➔ Refers to <i>personal skills necessary to establish and maintain relationships</i>.\n• <b>'their'</b> (Para 9, Line 3) ➔ Refers to <i>adolescents / people possessing life skills</i>.\n• <b>'this'</b> (Para 11, Line 7) ➔ Refers to <i>the innate / genetic need for belonging</i>.\n• <b>'it'</b> (Para 13, Line 2) ➔ Refers to <i>the ability / sense of belonging</i>.\n\n---\n❤️\n<b>Dear EthioFresh Students! All passage-based vocabulary terms, contextual definitions, and pronoun references for Unit 1 are now complete. Practice these terms for your reading comprehension exams! 🙏✨</b>"
